# tools/ales_reverb_tone_witness.rish — the tone: the reverb's DARKNESS knob, the high-cut on the reverberant tail (ALES198).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES198.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-104206_fill-ales198-lotus-reverb-tone.md
# Stands on: lotus/reverb_preset.rye (ALES190 — the named-room wet, run over the span unchanged); lotus/tone.rye (ALES40 — the one-pole low-pass and
# its precheck, the darkening applied to the wet span); lotus/clock.rye (ALES5 — the sample clock, converting the span); lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 —
# the Clip, its max_clip bounding the span).
#
# lotus/reverb_tone.rye gives the reverb its DARKNESS knob — the high-cut on the reverberant tail, what makes the room absorb highs the way a real
# space does (the highs die first, the low wash lingers). It reverberates the span wet in place through ALES190's reverb_preset UNCHANGED, then rolls
# the treble off the wet with ALES40's proven one-pole low-pass: tone_num == tone_den is the identity (a full-open tone), a smaller fraction darker.
# It invents no new audio arithmetic — every darkened sample is written by a proven stage — and it prechecks the tone coefficient BEFORE the wet is
# written, so a bad tone leaves the clip DRY (atomic). The selftest proves: at tone_num == tone_den the span equals reverb_preset byte for byte; for
# a sub-unity coefficient reverb_tone equals reverb_preset then tone.low_pass byte for byte and the darkened tail differs from the un-darkened wet;
# the darkened tail's high-frequency roughness is strictly less than the wet's; silence stays silence; and the faults forward atomic (an illegal
# tone coefficient BadTone with the clip still dry, a span past the clip DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange). Purely local — one clip, a
# validated clock; no network, no keys, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_reverb_tone_witness.rish

say "ales-reverb-tone: Language EN — the reverb now carries WHICH room (ALES190), HOW MUCH (ALES192/193 wet/dry), and WHEN (ALES194–197 pre-delay). The axis left is HOW BRIGHT: a real room absorbs high frequencies as the sound bounces, so its reverberant tail is always DARKER than the source — the highs die first and the low wash lingers. Every console names this knob (damping · high-cut · tone · colour); without it a reverb rings metallic. This rung is that knob."
say "ales-reverb-tone: Style Radiant — the identity edge is stated positively and proven exact: at tone_num == tone_den the span equals reverb_preset byte for byte (a full-open tone is exactly the un-darkened reverb); a smaller fraction rolls off more treble, and the darkened tail's high-frequency roughness is strictly less than the wet's — the tail darkens the way a real space does. The coefficient is a plain fraction, so it means the same roll-off at any sample rate."
say "ales-reverb-tone: Lens TAME — one clip of i16 PCM, a validated clock, and ALES190's fixed banks; NO new audio arithmetic — the tone is ALES40's proven one-pole low-pass over the proven wet, so no value is recomputed or can wrap; ReverbToneError = reverb_preset.ReverbPresetError || BadTone, the tone coefficient PRECHECKED before the wet is written so a BadTone leaves the clip dry (atomic), DurationTooLong / BadRange / BadTone by name before any write; siloed to lotus/, no network, no keys, no funds, no device. A wet-tail tone; a true in-loop Freeverb damping (a low-pass inside each comb's feedback) is named as a separate horizon, not claimed."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/reverb_tone"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/reverb_tone.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-reverb-tone: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/reverb_tone.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-reverb-tone: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-reverb-tone: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-reverb-tone' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-tone: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-reverb-tone line"

# This rung runs ALES190's reverb_preset over its public API for the wet — re-prove the named-room preset still stands green beside it.
let preset = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/reverb_preset.rye -femit-bin=lotus/bin/reverb_preset && lotus/bin/reverb_preset selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-reverb-preset' && echo yes"]
assert (trim preset.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-tone: the ALES190 named-room preset regressed under its tone"

# And re-prove ALES40's one-pole low-pass still stands green — the darkening it applies to the wet.
let toneg = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/tone.rye -femit-bin=lotus/bin/tone && lotus/bin/tone selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-tone' && echo yes"]
assert (trim toneg.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-tone: the ALES40 one-pole low-pass regressed under its reverb tone"

say "ales-reverb-tone: GREEN — the tone: the reverb's DARKNESS knob, the high-cut on the reverberant tail. Reverberate the span wet in place through ALES190's reverb_preset unchanged, then roll the treble off the wet with ALES40's proven one-pole low-pass — tone_num == tone_den the identity, a smaller fraction darker — no new audio arithmetic, every darkened sample written by a proven stage. Proven: at tone_num == tone_den exactly reverb_preset byte for byte; for a sub-unity coefficient reverb_tone equals reverb_preset then tone.low_pass byte for byte and the darkened tail differs from the un-darkened wet; the darkened tail's high-frequency roughness is strictly less than the wet's; silence stays silence; an illegal tone coefficient refuses BadTone with the clip still dry (prechecked before the wet is written), a span past the clip DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched; the ALES190 preset and the ALES40 low-pass stay GREEN beside it. A stereo tone (the same high-cut over both channels, one shared coefficient, the image held) is the thin twin next."
